# Pendaftaran Device Dokter — guided registration workflow

Canonical rules locked by DOCTOR-DEVICE-GUIDED-REGISTRATION-WORKFLOW-1.

## The workflow is a guide, never a second implementation

1. Every step except step 4 MUST post to the mutation route that already owns
   it (`settings.doctor-devices.store` / `.update` / `.approve-registration` /
   `.webauthn.options` / `.webauthn.store` / `.webauthn.revoke`). Do NOT add a
   convenience endpoint that duplicates a security-sensitive action.
2. Step 4 (`settings.doctor-device-registration.doctors.store`) is the ONLY new
   mutation. It calls `DoctorDeviceAuthorizationService::resolveOrRequest()`
   with `SOURCE_ADMIN` and can therefore only ever produce a PENDING row.
3. The workflow MUST NOT approve its own authorization request. Approval stays
   in Approval Device Dokter behind `manage_doctor_device_authorizations`
   (`DoctorDeviceAuthorizationPolicy::decide`).
4. `DoctorDeviceAuthorizationPolicy::requestForDevice` deliberately shares
   `manage_doctor_device_authorizations` with `decide`: a holder could already
   approve any pending row, so filing one grants nothing new. The invariant is
   enforced at the WRITE, not at the permission.
5. Return-to-workflow is a BOOLEAN (`registration_workflow`), never a URL, a
   path or a route name. Destinations are built from hardcoded route names.
   Do NOT add a `redirect_to=` parameter to a device endpoint.

## Two parties stay two parties

6. `register_doctor_devices` files a tablet; `manage_doctor_devices` enrols its
   credential and admits it into service. Do NOT widen either to make the
   wizard single-operator.
7. Where the current actor cannot act, render an explicit waiting state
   ("Menunggu Super Admin" / "Menunggu Approval Device Dokter"). The waiting
   state is presentation; every mutation re-authorizes server-side.
8. Prerequisites are enforced at the WRITE. A hand-typed URL may render a page,
   but the mutation MUST refuse (e.g. authorizing a doctor onto a tablet that
   is not yet approved).

## Derived state only

9. There MUST NOT be a `wizard_status` column or any stored workflow status.
   Every step is derived from existing security truth: the device row, its
   credentials, its status, its authorizations, the live proof, the readiness
   engine. A stored status drifts the moment a credential is revoked elsewhere.
10. Step 5 MUST have no control that can record a pass, and no route that
    could. The verdict comes from audit rows written by the login path itself,
    attributed to the device the assertion was performed on.

## Device-scoped readiness reuses fleet truth

11. `DoctorDeviceRegistrationReadinessService` MUST consume the existing
    `DoctorGlobalRolloutReadinessService::REASON_*` vocabulary,
    `DoctorDeviceIdentityProofPolicy`, `DoctorDeviceCredentialUsabilityPolicy`
    and `DoctorWebAuthnLiveProofService`. Do NOT re-derive readiness truth.
12. Credential usability lives in ONE place
    (`DoctorDeviceCredentialUsabilityPolicy`); the fleet engine delegates to it.
13. Every gate is evaluated — no early return. Revoking a credential, a device
    or an authorization must each fail its OWN gate and leave the others
    passing, or the operator is sent to fix the wrong page.
14. `UNVERIFIED` is NOT a pass. It means the gate could not be measured, and it
    must never be rendered or counted as green.
15. The estate-wide live-proof report is built ONCE per request. The board must
    judge every row against one snapshot, not one snapshot per row.

## GLOBAL SERVICE DISABLED != DEVICE DEFECT

16. **A globally disabled login capability MUST NOT be reported as a fault of
    the device.** When `DoctorAppLoginGate::deviceProofDenyReason()` returns
    `DENY_WEBAUTHN_LOGIN_DISABLED`, the estate-wide master switch
    (`doctor.pwa_webauthn_device_login`) is off and it refuses EVERY correctly
    provisioned tablet. The checklist MUST report
    `NO_INVALID_DEVICE_STATE = UNVERIFIED` carrying the real reason
    `webauthn_login_disabled`, and MUST NOT report it as `FAIL`, as
    `gate_refuses_a_provisioned_path`, or as a device/credential/authorization
    defect. The device-specific gates (device active, approval, credential,
    user verification, device binding, doctor authorization) MUST continue to
    report their own true state.

17. **A device MUST NOT be declared clinically READY while the required login
    capability is globally disabled.** `UNVERIFIED` is not a pass, so the
    verdict stays `NOT_READY` and step 7 stays blocked — no
    "READY FOR CLINICAL USE", by design. Fail-closed is deliberate: a tablet is
    not ready for clinical use if no doctor can log in on it.

    These two rules are ONE decision, not a contradiction. Rule 16 governs the
    REASON shown to the operator (do not send them to re-enrol a healthy
    tablet); rule 17 governs the VERDICT (do not promise a tablet works when
    the capability it needs is switched off). Changing either without the other
    reintroduces exactly one of the two failure modes.

18. Do NOT "fix" a NOT_READY board by turning a production flag on. The flag is
    an estate posture decision with its own change process.
